.TH SD 3
.SH NAME
sd \- storage devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #S /dev
.nf

.BI /dev/sd n /data
.BI /dev/sd n /ctl
.BI /dev/sd n /esp
.BI /dev/sd n /dos
\&...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I sd
device serves a two-level directory, one
.BI sd n
per disk the virtio block driver found, numbered from zero in the order
the driver attached them.
Each disk's directory holds
.B data
and
.BR ctl ,
and one file per partition the disk's master boot record names.
.PP
.B Data
is the whole disk, byte-addressable, read and write, with no
interpretation.
A partition file is a window onto the same bytes: it begins where its
partition begins and is as long as the partition is, and a read or a write
against it cannot reach past that range.
.PP
The name a partition gets comes from its type byte in the master boot
record:
.B esp
for an EFI system partition,
.B dos
for a FAT type,
.B plan9
for Plan 9's own type, and
.BI part N
for anything else, numbered from one in table order.
A sector zero that begins with a jump instruction is a FAT boot sector
rather than a master boot record, and is not read as one.
A disk with neither gets no partitions, only
.BR data .
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
answers one line:
.IP
.EX
geometry 20971520 512
.EE
.PP
the sector count, then the sector size in bytes.
Writing to
.B ctl
fails with
.BR EPERM :
there is nothing on this device yet for a client to set.
.PP
.B #S
is bound at
.B /dev
as a second member, after
.BR #c ,
so a listing of
.B /dev
shows every disk's directory beside the console's own files.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/sd
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR cons (3),
.IR bind (1),
.IR stat (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Opening a disk or a partition that is not there fails with
.BR ENXIO .
Writing to
.B ctl
fails with
.BR EPERM .
Reading or writing a directory fails with
.B EISDIR
or
.BR ENOTDIR .
